content.text:
Found it. It's a scrolling bug in the page, not missing colour. In `rust/reva-web/www/index.html`, coloured output is added in two steps:

```js
const appendOut = (s) => {
    const runs = ansi.feed(s);
    if (runs.length === 1 && !runs[0][1]) return append(runs[0][0]);
    const span = append("");          // ← append() scrolls to the bottom HERE, while the span is empty
    for (const [t, c] of runs) { ... span.append(r); }   // ← the coloured text is added after the scroll
    return span;
};
```

`append()` sets `out.scrollTop = out.scrollHeight` before the coloured runs exist, so the view stops at the end of the old output. Plain text is added in one step, which is why ordinary output scrolls correctly. The fractals listing is all coloured, so the whole `pp` output ends up below the visible area, as you saw. Scroll down in the REPL window and the colour listing is there.

You said you want fixes made in the cloud and verified here, so I haven't changed anything. Here's a note you can pass to the cloud agent:

> **pp output doesn't auto-scroll in the web/desktop REPL.** In `rust/reva-web/www/index.html`, `appendOut()` calls `append("")`, which scrolls to the bottom, and only then adds the coloured child spans, so the view stops above the new output. Plain output is fine because it goes through `append(text)` in one step. Fix: in `appendOut`, set `out.scrollTop = out.scrollHeight` after the loop that adds the runs. Reproduce in the desktop app with `needs highlight/core` then `pp turtle/fractals`; the window stays at the prompt above the listing. This affects the browser page and the desktop app (desktop/dist is copied from www).

Once that's merged, I'll pull, rebuild the desktop app and check that the window ends at the bottom of the listing.

Yes. A REPL should keep the newest output and the prompt in view, and the page is meant to: every plain write scrolls to the bottom. Coloured output is the one path that skips it, because the scroll runs before the text is added. That's a bug, not a design choice.

A sturdier fix than the one-line move is to scroll in a single place, after each batch of output is written:

```js
const flush = () => {
  frame = 0;
  if (pending) appendOut(pending);
  pending = "";
  out.scrollTop = out.scrollHeight;   // once, after the content exists
};
```

Then no output path can forget to scroll.

One refinement most terminals have: stay at the bottom only if the user was already there. If you've scrolled up to read earlier output while something is still printing, it shouldn't yank you down on every line. That means checking whether the view was at the bottom before writing (`out.scrollHeight - out.scrollTop - out.clientHeight < a few px`), and scrolling only then. Typing a new line should always jump back to the bottom.

Updated note for the cloud agent:

> **REPL page: keep the view at the bottom.** In `rust/reva-web/www/index.html`, `appendOut()` scrolls inside `append("")` before adding the coloured spans, so `pp` output (all coloured) ends up below the visible area. Fix: remove the scroll from `append()` and scroll once in `flush()` after the output is added. Only scroll if the view was at the bottom before the write, so a user who scrolled up to read isn't pulled down, and always scroll to the bottom when a line is submitted. Reproduce in the desktop app with `needs highlight/core` then `pp turtle/fractals`. Affects the browser page and the desktop app.

When it lands, I'll pull, rebuild and check both that `pp` ends at the bottom and that scrolling up during long output stays put.

